    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nCorrect the guidance for disabling the GPT-OSS Python tool: `VLLM_GPT_OSS_SYSTEM_TOOL_MCP_LABELS` filters MCP labels, while native Responses tool types can still select registered tools.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Distinguishes tool-server registration from MCP label mapping.\n- Explains that omitting an MCP label does not disable the corresponding native tool type.\n- Documents how to exclude the Python namespace at the tool-server level, or disable server-side tool execution entirely.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `pre-commit run --files docs/usage/security.md` — all applicable hooks passed.\n- `pre-commit run markdownlint-cli2 --files docs/usage/security.md` — passed.\n- `pre-commit run typos --files docs/usage/security.md` — passed.\n- `git diff --check` — passed.\n- Checked the guidance against `extract_tool_types()`, the registered-tool checks in Responses serving, and tool-server registration. A helper-level reproduction with OpenAI SDK 3.8.0 confirmed that empty and container-only MCP allowlists exclude MCP Python/browser labels but preserve native tool selection.\n- Model evaluations are not applicable: this change only updates documentation and does not change serving behavior.\n\n## Details\n\nThe existing instruction says that omitting `code_interpreter` disables Python, but that variable only filters `mcp` entries. Native `code_interpreter` requests can still select a registered Python tool. The replacement describes the actual configuration needed to avoid registering that capability.\n\nThis corrects deployment guidance introduced in #35139. Searches of open PRs for the variable name, MCP tool security documentation, tool availability, and disabling Python found no existing PR making this correction. The browser-backend work in #61288 changes backend selection, not the scope of MCP label filtering.\n\nAI assistance (OpenAI Codex) was used to investigate the behavior and prepare this documentation change.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
